.TH IBV_RU_RMA 1 "August 8, 2026" "libibverbs" "USER COMMANDS"

.SH NAME
ibv_ru_rma \- simple RU remote memory access test

.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ibv_ru_rma
[\-p port] [\-d device] [\-i ib port] [\-s size] [\-m size]
[\-r rx depth] [\-n iters] [\-e] [\-g gid index] [\-c] [\-t test]
[\-Q] [\-D] [\-A] [\-J] [\-u rkey] [\-b] [\-j jobid] [\-W] [\-R]
[\-S sges] [\-\-pidonfep n] [\-\-ri n] \fBHOSTNAME\fR

.B ibv_ru_rma
[\-p port] [\-d device] [\-i ib port] [\-s size] [\-m size]
[\-r rx depth] [\-n iters] [\-e] [\-g gid index] [\-c] [\-t test]
[\-Q] [\-D] [\-A] [\-J] [\-u rkey] [\-b] [\-j jobid] [\-W] [\-R]
[\-S sges] [\-\-pidonfep n] [\-\-ri n]

.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
Run a remote memory access using the reliable unconnected (RU) transport,
exercising RDMA read and write with 32- and 64-bit memory keys and
with 32- and 64-bit immediate data.
.PP
Invoked without a
.I HOSTNAME
the program acts as the server and waits for a connection; invoked with
one it acts as the client and connects to the server at that address.
.PP
Beyond the data transfers the program exercises the Ultra Ethernet
memory region and addressing features. This includes plain, derived,
job-restricted, and application-assigned-RKey memory regions. Naming
the peer either by extended address handle or through the job address
table. Peer access using relative or absolute addressing. See
.BR ibv_uet (7)
for the underlying model.
.PP
The device must report
.I IBV_DEVICE_RU
and
.I IBV_DEVICE_KEY64
in
.IR device_cap_flags_ex .

.SH OPTIONS

.PP
.TP
\fB\-p\fR, \fB\-\-port\fR=\fIPORT\fR
use TCP port \fIPORT\fR for initial synchronization (default 18515)
.TP
\fB\-d\fR, \fB\-\-ib\-dev\fR=\fIDEVICE\fR
use IB device \fIDEVICE\fR (default first device found)
.TP
\fB\-i\fR, \fB\-\-ib\-port\fR=\fIPORT\fR
use port \fIPORT\fR of the IB device (default 1)
.TP
\fB\-s\fR, \fB\-\-size\fR=\fISIZE\fR
transfer messages of size \fISIZE\fR
.TP
\fB\-m\fR, \fB\-\-mtu\fR=\fISIZE\fR
path MTU \fISIZE\fR (default 1024)
.TP
\fB\-r\fR, \fB\-\-rx\-depth\fR=\fIDEPTH\fR
post \fIDEPTH\fR receives at a time
.TP
\fB\-n\fR, \fB\-\-iters\fR=\fIITERS\fR
perform \fIITERS\fR exchanges
.TP
\fB\-e\fR, \fB\-\-events\fR
sleep while waiting for work completion events (default is to poll for
completions)
.TP
\fB\-g\fR, \fB\-\-gid\-idx\fR=\fIGIDINDEX\fR
use local port GID index \fIGIDINDEX\fR. The GID type selects the
address family used on the wire; see
.BR ibv_query_gid_ex (3)
.TP
\fB\-c\fR, \fB\-\-chk\fR
validate received buffer
.TP
\fB\-t\fR, \fB\-\-test\fR=\fITEST\fR
run test \fITEST\fR: \fIrma\fR uses the legacy post send path,
\fIrma_ex\fR the work request builders, and \fIrma_ex64\fR the builders
with 64-bit immediate data (default \fIrma_ex64\fR)
.TP
\fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-\-auto\-qpn\fR
let the provider assign the queue pair number rather than supplying
one. A provider-assigned queue pair number always uses relative
addressing. Cannot be combined with \fB\-\-pidonfep\fR or \fB\-\-ri\fR
.TP
\fB\-D\fR, \fB\-\-derive\-mr\fR
transfer using a memory region derived from the registered one
.TP
\fB\-A\fR, \fB\-\-addr\-table\fR
name the peer through the job address table rather than with an
extended address handle; see
.BR ibv_insert_addr (3)
.TP
\fB\-J\fR, \fB\-\-job\-mr\fR
register a job-restricted memory region, reachable only by transfers
bearing a matching job.
.TP
\fB\-u\fR, \fB\-\-user\-rkey\fR=\fIKEY\fR
register the memory region with the application-assigned RKey
\fIKEY\fR. Requires \fIIBV_DEVICE_USER_RKEY\fR
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-absolute\fR
use absolute addressing, in which the server accepts requests
irrespective of the sender's JobID. The two sides are normally given
distinct JobIDs with \fB\-\-job\-id\fR
.TP
\fB\-j\fR, \fB\-\-job\-id\fR=\fIID\fR
use \fIID\fR as this side's JobID
.TP
\fB\-W\fR, \fB\-\-write32\fR
RDMA write with a 32-bit RKey rather than the default 64-bit RKey
.TP
\fB\-R\fR, \fB\-\-read32\fR
RDMA read with a 32-bit RKey rather than the default 64-bit RKey
.TP
\fB\-S\fR, \fB\-\-sge\fR=\fIN\fR
scatter/gather the local buffer across \fIN\fR entries for RMA read
and write
.TP
\fB\-\-pidonfep\fR=\fIN\fR
use \fIN\fR as the PIDonFEP field of this side's queue pair number
.TP
\fB\-\-ri\fR=\fIN\fR
use \fIN\fR as the resource index field of this side's queue pair
number

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ibv_ru_pingpong (1),
